<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Window
    x:Class="Rudis.Shell.MainWindow"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:regions="using:Rudis.Shell.Regions">

    <!-- Region stack (CLAUDE.md rule 7 — the names are the handoff's, verbatim):
         TitleBar (40px, plan 50-05) · Toolbar (52px, plan 50-05) · body.

         The body is the handoff's TOP ROW of its 2x2 functional grid (README:55-80):
         a FIXED 486px left column (top-left = Canvas) and a flexing right column
         (top-right = Preview above Transport). Both columns are defined HERE, in one
         place, so plan 51-05 fills column 0 without restructuring this grid a second
         time.

           · column 0 — `Canvas` (plan 51-05, SHELL-04): the handoff's dotted-grid
             stage, its `Canvas › ToolPalette` and its zoom chip, above the
             diagnostic readout. The palette drives the ink layer that sits over the
             Preview swapchain — see Canvas.xaml.cs.
           · column 1 — `Preview` (plan 51-04, REAL SwapChainPanel) + `Transport`
             (44px, plan 50-06) docked to its bottom, per README:118.
           · column 2 — the labelled `Inspector` placeholder, re-owned by Phase 55.

         The body is now the FULL 2x2 grid. Column 0 holds `Canvas` + `Chat`
         (plan 54-05, split by a draggable divider) / `Shell.StatusText` / `MediaBin`
         (plan 53-02) and column 1 holds `Preview` / `Transport` / `Timeline` (plan
         52-06); the two bottom rows are pinned to the same height so the cross-divider
         reads as one line (README:75).

         The status readout still renders the REAL mirrored backend state 50-03/50-04
         put there (probe envelope, cursors, resync count, both poll rates' liveness,
         and now the Preview attach measurement) — it is not a placeholder for absent
         data.

         ADDENDUM quick-260731-w45 (2026-07-31): column 0's middle row is NO LONGER A
         VISIBLE READOUT. It is a ZERO-HEIGHT row that still HOLDS `Shell.StatusText`,
         which stays in the visual tree purely so UIA can read it. Everything the
         paragraph above says about the readout's CONTENT is unchanged and still true —
         `RefreshStatus` is byte-untouched and `.Name` still carries every diagnostic
         line in full. What changed is that no human sees it: its ~159 logical px went
         to row 0, taking the Canvas/Chat split from ~113 to ~193 logical px per side
         at the 50/50 default. Full before/after in this task's
         `artifacts/w45-uia-probe.md`. -->
    <!-- AllowDrop on the ROOT, not on a region: the handoff says import accepts a
         file drop "anywhere on the window" (README:92). DragOver/Drop route into the
         Toolbar's single shared import routine (T-50-20's mitigation lives there and
         in the handler below). -->
    <Grid x:Name="WindowRoot"
          Background="{StaticResource bg-window}"
          AllowDrop="True"
          DragOver="OnWindowDragOver"
          Drop="OnWindowDrop">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <regions:TitleBar x:Name="TitleBarRegion" Grid.Row="0" />
        <regions:Toolbar x:Name="ToolbarRegion" Grid.Row="1" />

        <Grid Grid.Row="2">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <!-- handoff: fixed left column, 486px (README:76). Canvas, plan 51-05. -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="486" />
                <!-- Preview + Transport — flexes to fill the remaining width. -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <!-- Inspector placeholder, Phase 53. Auto = zero while collapsed. -->
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <!-- Column 0 is the handoff's Canvas quadrant, and plan 51-05 FILLS it.

                 Two rows rather than one overlay: the diagnostic readout keeps the
                 whole column's width (see the NoWrap note below — narrowing it
                 further has a MEASURED cost) while the Canvas keeps the handoff's
                 own geometry, with the ToolPalette top-left and the zoom chip
                 bottom-left exactly where README:96 puts them. Layering the readout
                 ON TOP of the region would have collided with both.

                 The readout still renders the REAL mirrored backend state 50-03/50-04
                 put there — probe envelope, cursors, resync count, both poll rates'
                 liveness — it is not a placeholder for absent data. -->
            <Grid Grid.Column="0">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="*" />
                    <!-- quick-260731-w45: was Height="Auto" (~159 logical px of visible
                         diagnostics). Now ZERO — the row still HOLDS `Shell.StatusText`,
                         it just gives it no height. See the ⚠ block on that element. -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="0" />
                    <!-- Plan 53-02's ONE row. See the MediaBin note below. -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="322" MinHeight="140" />
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                <!-- D-01 (owner deviation, 54-CONTEXT): Chat is DOCKED, not floating.
                     The Canvas row SPLITS — Canvas above, Chat below, 50/50 by default
                     (D-02), never persisted (D-03). This voids the handoff's
                     floating/drag/clamp/shadow/minimize Chat clauses
                     (README:144-145,175,234 + the Chat.MinimizeButton atom, D-04) and
                     eliminates the whole z-order/hole-punch risk class v6.0 needed
                     (index.html:335-338) and Phase 51 deleted (SHELL-04): a docked Chat
                     never overlaps the SwapChainPanel.

                     The divider is HAND-ROLLED (Regions/Chat/ChatSplit.cs) — GridSplitter
                     is NOT a built-in WinUI 3 control; see 54-01's recorded Option-B
                     decision, which is settled and must not be re-opened here.

                     SPLITTING THIS ROW DOES NOT MOVE THE 322 CROSS-DIVIDER BELOW, and
                     that is the claim plan 54-05's UIA proof measures rather than
                     asserts: `Shell.StatusText` and the `MediaBin` row still own rows 1
                     and 2 of this column, and the divider's Y is pinned by the MediaBin
                     row's FIXED height, not by anything inside this row.

                     ⚠ THE TWO MinHeights ARE FLOORS AGAINST DEGENERATE LAYOUT, NOT
                     COMFORT TARGETS — and the difference was MEASURED, at some cost.

                     They are this plan's numbers, not the handoff's (whose Chat sizing
                     clauses — a 300x332 float — are void under D-01). 96 is the smallest
                     Chat in which BOTH the 36px header and the ~44px composer are fully
                     on screen with a non-zero transcript above it; 80 is the smallest
                     Canvas that still shows its 38px ToolPalette and its 29px zoom chip.
                     Below those the rows are not merely cramped, they LOSE CONTROLS.

                     WHY NOT 54-03's RECOMMENDED 160-180 CHAT FLOOR, which is the right
                     number for a COMFORTABLE transcript: it does not fit in this column
                     at the shell's actual default window height, and the failure is
                     silent-then-total. MEASURED on this machine (5120x1440 display at
                     125%, work area 1390px; the shell opens NOT maximized at 3072x813
                     LOGICAL px): column 0's body is 721 logical px, of which the MediaBin
                     row takes a fixed 322 and `Shell.StatusText`'s nine diagnostic lines
                     take ~159 — leaving 226 logical px for the Canvas, this divider and
                     the Chat TOGETHER. With a 176 floor the two minimums cannot both fit,
                     so (a) `ChatSplit.Apply`'s over-constrained guard refuses every drag —
                     measured, a 60px pull moved the divider 0px — and (b) the Grid clipped
                     the LAST row's overflow, putting `Chat.Composer` and `Chat.SendButton`
                     OFFSCREEN (`IsOffscreen=true`), i.e. a Chat you cannot type into.
                     Both readings are in 54-05's SUMMARY.

                     The comfort number is delivered by the DEFAULT, not by the floor:
                     two equal `*` rows put ~113px on each side here, and at the handoff's
                     own 910-tall reference artboard they put ~165px on each side — which
                     is 54-03's band. The floors only ever bind on a window too short for
                     the column, and THAT is a window-geometry problem this file already
                     assigns to a future owner (see the Timeline note's MEASURED LIMIT
                     paragraph, same shape, same reason). Recorded for 54-06 rather than
                     fixed here: raising the window's default height would move every
                     region and re-baseline the capture and GC suites.

                     ── ADDENDUM quick-260731-w45 (2026-07-31) — THE BUDGET ABOVE IS NOW
                        HISTORY. READ THESE NUMBERS FOR THE SHELL THAT SHIPS. ──

                     The 226 figure is what column 0 had while `Shell.StatusText` was a
                     VISIBLE ~159-logical-px readout. That row is now ZERO-HEIGHT (see the
                     RowDefinition above and the ⚠ block on the element itself), and the
                     `*` row 0 is the only flexible row in this column, so it absorbed
                     every reclaimed pixel. MEASURED on the same machine, same 3072x814
                     LOGICAL window — no window-geometry change was made:

                       row 1 (`Shell.StatusText`)   159.2 -> 0     logical
                       row 0 (Canvas+divider+Chat)  234.4 -> 393.6 logical  (+159.2)
                       Canvas side @ 50/50 default  114.4 -> 193.6 logical
                       Chat   side @ 50/50 default  112.0 -> 192.0 logical
                       MediaBin row top             UNCHANGED (rel-window 605 both runs)
                       divider travel, 60px pull    clamped (~41 ceiling) -> full 60px

                     So the 50/50 DEFAULT now delivers ~189-194 logical px per side, i.e.
                     54-03's 160-180 comfort band, at the shell's real default height —
                     the thing the paragraph above could only get from a 910-tall artboard.

                     THE FLOORS STAY 80 / 96, and that is a decision taken FROM this
                     measurement, not inertia. Raising them buys nothing (the default
                     already clears 160 by ~30px) and costs the exact failure pair 54-05
                     measured: a higher floor sum widens the window-height range in which
                     `ChatSplit.Apply`'s over-constrained guard freezes the divider AND
                     WinUI clips `Chat.Composer` offscreen. Floors guard degenerate layout;
                     the default carries comfort. Unchanged for that reason.

                     There is NO stale cap to remove anywhere in the split math: ChatSplit's
                     `lo`/`hi` derive from the LIVE total, so freed space reaches both sides
                     automatically. Verified by the +79.2 / +80.0 per-side split above.
                     Full readings: this task's `artifacts/w45-uia-probe.md`.

                     Both numbers are READ OFF these RowDefinitions by the drag handlers
                     (MainWindow.xaml.cs), so the Grid's clamp and ChatSplit's clamp are
                     one number each and cannot drift apart.

                     D-05's TAB ORDER, MEASURED IN THE RUNNING SHELL rather than assumed
                     (see the measurement note below the divider). -->
                <Grid Grid.Row="0" x:Name="CanvasChatSplit">
                    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                        <RowDefinition x:Name="CanvasSplitRow" Height="*" MinHeight="80" />
                        <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                        <RowDefinition x:Name="ChatSplitRow" Height="*" MinHeight="96" />
                    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                    <regions:Canvas x:Name="CanvasRegion" Grid.Row="0" />

                    <Border x:Name="ChatSplitter" Grid.Row="1" Height="6"
                            Background="{StaticResource bg-bar}"
                            BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                            BorderThickness="0,1,0,1"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Chat.Splitter"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="Resize canvas and chat"
                            PointerPressed="OnChatSplitterPressed"
                            PointerMoved="OnChatSplitterMoved"
                            PointerReleased="OnChatSplitterReleased"
                            PointerCanceled="OnChatSplitterReleased"
                            PointerCaptureLost="OnChatSplitterReleased" />

                    <regions:Chat x:Name="ChatRegion" Grid.Row="2" />
                </Grid>

                <!-- D-05's TAB ORDER — MEASURED IN THE RUNNING SHELL, not assumed, and
                     deliberately NOT re-engineered.

                     The handoff's intent (README:162, kept VERBATIM by owner decision) is
                     Toolbar -> Canvas -> Chat -> Preview/Transport -> MediaBin -> Timeline.
                     40 real TAB presses against a real launch traverse:

                       Toolbar.ImportButton -> Toolbar.UndoButton -> Toolbar.RedoButton
                       -> Toolbar.ModeTabs.Edit -> Canvas.ToolPalette.Pointer
                       -> Chat.KeyButton -> Chat.Composer -> Chat.SendButton
                       -> MediaBin.ImportMediaButton -> MediaBin.ImportFolderButton
                       -> MediaBin.Breadcrumb -> Preview.ProgramTab
                       -> Transport.FrameMenu -> Timeline.Root -> Timeline.AddTrackButton
                       -> (wraps)

                     i.e. Toolbar -> Canvas -> CHAT -> MediaBin -> Preview/Transport ->
                     Timeline: the default tree order reads straight DOWN column 0 and then
                     down column 1, which is exactly the reading D-05's own text blesses.
                     The ONE difference from the handoff is MediaBin before Preview — a
                     CROSS-COLUMN ordering that predates this phase, is a pure consequence
                     of the 2x2 grid's declaration order, and would need TabIndex plumbing
                     across four regions to change. That is not Chat scope, and no TabIndex
                     is added here: what D-01 had to get right is that Chat lands
                     IMMEDIATELY AFTER Canvas, which it does, because it is the next
                     element in the same column. Also confirmed: the 6px divider is not a
                     tab stop (it is a Border, not a control), so adding it cost the
                     keyboard user nothing. -->


                <!-- ⚠ `Shell.StatusText` KEEPS its AutomationId across this move —
                     Phase 50's published-output smoke test, plan 50-09's UIA probe and
                     plan 51-06 all read it by that id.

                     ⚠ NoWrap + ellipsis is LOAD-BEARING, not cosmetic. Phase 50 wrapped
                     this block across the FULL window width; in a 486px column the same
                     text re-flows to roughly four times the line count, and it is rebuilt
                     and re-laid-out on every mirror notification (~100/s during playback).
                     That measurably reddened Phase 50's
                     `no_gen0_collections_during_sustained_playback_with_uia_attached`.
                     Nothing is hidden by this: UIA reads the TEXT, not the layout, so
                     `Shell.StatusText`'s Name still carries every line in full — which is
                     how 50-09's probe and plan 51-06 read it.

                     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
                     ⚠⚠ quick-260731-w45 — THIS ELEMENT IS INTENTIONALLY INVISIBLE AND
                        MUST STAY `Visibility="Visible"`. THAT IS NOT A CONTRADICTION.
                     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

                     It is hidden by its LAYOUT SLOT, not by its visibility: its row is
                     `Height="0"` (see the RowDefinition above) and it carries
                     `Opacity="0"`. It is still in the visual tree, so it still has an
                     automation peer — and the peer is the entire point. Its
                     BoundingRectangle is now `{0,0,0,0}` and `IsOffscreen=true`, both
                     MEASURED on a real launch, both fine.

                     ⚠ `Visibility="Collapsed"` IS THE KNOWN-WRONG MOVE. A collapsed
                     element leaves the visual tree and has no automation peer, so it
                     resolves `(not found)` — measured in this same task, where eight
                     peerless layout containers all read `(not found)` by id. That
                     BREAKS `ChatUiaTests.cs:115` outright. Do not "tidy" this element
                     by collapsing it. Same for deleting it, and same for gating the
                     `RefreshStatus` assignment that feeds its Name.

                     THREE THINGS MUST HOLD SIMULTANEOUSLY, and each has a live consumer:

                       1. FINDABLE BY AUTOMATIONID — `ChatUiaTests.cs:115` asserts
                          `FindElement("Shell.StatusText")` is not null.
                       2. A READABLE, STABLE `BoundingRectangle.Top` —
                          `ChatUiaTests.cs:331-334` reads it before and after a real
                          divider drag and asserts it moved 0px (tolerance 2). A fixed
                          `{0,0,0,0}` satisfies this; instability would not.
                       3. `.Name` CARRYING EVERY DIAGNOSTIC LINE IN FULL —
                          `InkHandoffTests.cs:224`, `LayeringProofTests.cs:360` and
                          `PreviewSurfaceTests.cs:155` read `status.Name` and write it
                          into COMMITTED evidence artifacts under
                          `.planning/phases/51-*/artifacts/`. For a TextBlock, `.Name`
                          comes from `Text` — which is why `RefreshStatus`'s
                          `StatusText.Text = full` assignment must stay byte-identical.

                     Those three reads are NON-ASSERTIVE in two of the three suites: if
                     the peer ever disappears they will quietly record
                     "(Shell.StatusText not found)" into the evidence files without any
                     test going red. That is why the invariant is written HERE, at the
                     element, and why w45 verified the regenerated artifact's CONTENT
                     rather than just a green run.

                     `Opacity="0"` is DEFENCE IN DEPTH, NOT THE MECHANISM. A control run
                     with the zero-height row and NO opacity produced an equally invisible
                     panel (WinUI clips the NoWrap text to its zero-height slot) and
                     byte-identical UIA readings. It is kept because it costs nothing, it
                     is UIA-inert, and it states the intent next to the invariant. Both
                     runs are in the task's `artifacts/w45-uia-probe.md`. -->
                <TextBlock x:Name="StatusText"
                           Grid.Row="1"
                           Text="starting…"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource text-primary}"
                           FontFamily="Calibri"
                           FontSize="13"
                           Margin="16"
                           Opacity="0"
                           TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Shell.StatusText" />

                <!-- `MediaBin` - the handoff's BOTTOM-LEFT quadrant (plan 53-02,
                     SHELL-03), the real virtualized tile grid over mirrored state.

                     THE HEIGHT IS COPIED, NOT CHOSEN. The handoff requires the 2x2
                     grid's cross-divider to read as ONE clean line (README:75). Column 0
                     and Column 1 are independent nested Grids, so the only way to honor
                     that is to give this row the SAME height Column 1's Timeline row
                     has: both columns then pin their bottom row to the same height, so
                     both dividers land at the same Y regardless of window height. 322 /
                     140 were READ OFF Column 1's third RowDefinition at execution time
                     (Phase 52 owns that number and was live on this file); if it ever
                     moves, this must move with it, or the cross breaks silently.

                     WHY `Shell.StatusText` IS STILL HERE, HIDDEN - the obvious review
                     question, restated by quick-260731-w45 (2026-07-31).

                     HISTORY (still true, and the reason the element sits in this row at
                     all): it never had to MOVE when plan 53-02 took this bottom row. This
                     row is bottom-pinned at a fixed height and the Canvas's row is `*`, so
                     the readout ate from the flexible space ABOVE it and the 322
                     cross-divider's Y was unaffected either way.

                     NOW: w45 removed the readout from VIEW — its row is `Height="0"` and
                     the TextBlock carries `Opacity="0"` — and the ~159 logical px it used
                     to occupy went to the `*` row above. The element itself was NOT
                     deleted and NOT collapsed, because the same UIA consumers still read
                     it: Phase 50's published-output smoke test, plan 50-09's probe, plan
                     51-06, `ChatUiaTests` (presence + rect stability) and three suites
                     that write its `.Name` into committed evidence artifacts. The full
                     invariant, and why `Visibility="Collapsed"` is the known-wrong move,
                     is at the element itself — see the ⚠⚠ block above it.

                     WHAT THIS ROW DID NOT DO IS MOVE: MEASURED before and after w45, the
                     MediaBin row's top edge sits at the identical window-relative y (605
                     physical, from `MediaBin.Header`; `MediaBin.RegionTag` 619 in both).
                     The cross-divider still reads as one clean line, and this row, its
                     `Height="322" MinHeight="140"` and the element below are byte-
                     untouched by that task.

                     CROSS-PHASE CONTRACT, same shape as the Timeline note below: Phase
                     51 owns this file's SHAPE, Phase 52 owns Column 1's Timeline row,
                     and Phase 53 owns THIS ROW and nothing else in this file. -->
                <regions:MediaBin x:Name="MediaBinRegion" Grid.Row="2" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- Preview + Transport. The handoff docks Transport to the BOTTOM of
                 Preview (README:118), and that docking contract is what plan 50-06
                 made real: two rows, Preview flexing above a fixed 44px bar, both on
                 the SAME `bg-preview` background — which is also why swapping the panel
                 above for a real SwapChainPanel changed nothing in this bar.

                 Preview's ~220px min height is the handoff's (README:174).

                 ⚠ Preview is now a REAL Rust-owned swapchain surface (plan 51-04,
                 SHELL-04). Phase 50's labelled placeholder is DELETED, not hidden.
                 There is still no child-window hosting and no airspace workaround of
                 any kind here — the ink overlay, dialogs and the Canvas are ordinary
                 XAML siblings in this same visual tree, which is the entire point of
                 the phase. A grep for the Win32 window-parenting/region APIs over
                 shell/ returns zero and that is a mechanical gate (plan 51-01). -->
            <Grid Grid.Column="1">
                <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                    <RowDefinition Height="*" MinHeight="220" />
                    <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                    <!-- Plan 52-06's ONE row. See the Timeline note below. -->
                    <RowDefinition Height="322" MinHeight="140" />
                </Grid.RowDefinitions>

                <regions:Preview x:Name="PreviewRegion" Grid.Row="0" />
                <regions:Transport x:Name="TransportRegion" Grid.Row="1" />

                <!-- `Timeline` - the handoff's BOTTOM-RIGHT quadrant (plan 52-06,
                     SHELL-05), the real custom-drawn, viewport-culled clip surface.

                     CROSS-PHASE CONTRACT, stated because both phases were live on
                     this file at once: Phase 51 owns this file's SHAPE - the 2x2 grid,
                     the Canvas column, the Preview/Transport subtree's internals.
                     Phase 52 owns this ROW and nothing else in this file. The edit is
                     purely additive for that reason: one RowDefinition and one element.

                     THE ROW SPLIT. The handoff's default is a ~452px top row with the
                     rest below (README:173), and it is meant to be draggable. 322px is
                     that default expressed from the other end, and it is EXACT at the
                     reference artboard: 910 window - 40 TitleBar - 52 Toolbar - 44
                     Transport - 322 = 452. Pinning the BOTTOM row rather than the top
                     one is what keeps this hunk additive - the Preview row's own `*`
                     and its 220px minimum (README:174) are untouched - and the
                     Timeline's own ~140px minimum is here so ruler + 1-2 lanes always
                     show. The draggable splitter itself is not this plan's.

                     MEASURED LIMIT, recorded rather than claimed past: that 140 holds
                     while the window has room for it. Below roughly a 630px-tall
                     window the grid is over-constrained (Preview's 220 + Transport's
                     44 + Timeline's 140 + 92 of chrome) and the Timeline is the row
                     that yields - measured at 132.8 logical px in a 620px window. A
                     window-level minimum size is the real fix and belongs to whichever
                     plan owns window geometry. -->
                <regions:Timeline x:Name="TimelineRegion" Grid.Row="2" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- Toolbar.ModeTabs = Color reveals THIS, and nothing more.

                 `Inspector` stays a DEFERRED surface. PHASE 53 DID NOT BUILD IT, and
                 that is a decision rather than an omission: v6.0 ships no Inspector at
                 all (`grep -rn -i inspector frontend/` = zero hits), so there was
                 nothing to be at parity with, and the handoff's Inspector spec
                 (README:149) is COLOUR GRADING, which CLAUDE.md defers to v2 and for
                 which no backend command exists. Owner decision D-01, 2026-07-30;
                 ROADMAP SC-1 was amended accordingly by plan 53-01 rather than
                 quietly under-delivered against.

                 Re-owned by PHASE 55's integrated parity gate, which is where the
                 nine-region claim gets decided against a real build.

                 Per CLAUDE.md rule 1 an explicitly-labelled placeholder is permitted
                 here where a fake grading UI is not: no sliders, no curves, nothing
                 implying an operation exists. -->
            <Border x:Name="InspectorPlaceholder"
                    Grid.Column="2"
                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                    Width="300"
                    Background="{StaticResource bg-bar}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource border-hairline}"
                    BorderThickness="1,0,0,0">
                <TextBlock Text="Inspector — deferred surface (Phase 55)"
                           Foreground="{StaticResource text-tertiary}"
                           FontFamily="Calibri"
                           FontSize="12"
                           Margin="16"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           TextAlignment="Center"
                           AutomationProperties.AutomationId="Inspector.Placeholder"
                           AutomationProperties.Name="Inspector, deferred surface" />
            </Border>
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</Window>
